{"model_id": "model_d3b03caa65b1", "model_name": "XunZi-M", "record_id": "update_2026-08-09__manual_recall_xunzi", "paper_title": "XunZi, an AI biologist, reveals disease-modifying targets", "routes": [{"route_id": "route_6ccccc72d8fe", "route_label": "Pan-cancer multi-omics feature learning", "lifecycle_phase": "pretraining", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "pan-cancer", "source_object_verbatim": "TCGA, CPTAC, GEO and literature-derived multi-omics datasets for pan-cancer", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["derived a feature vector from its expression profiles across 33 cancer types", "embedded each gene node in the graph", "trained a GCN to propagate features across the heterogeneous graph structure"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "gene-level expression vectors", "carrier_family": "dense_continuous_carrier", "carrier_subtype": "direct_projected_embedding", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "embedded with a feature vector derived from its expression profiles across 33 cancer types", "evidence_quote": "For pan-cancer, each gene node was embedded with a feature vector derived from its expression profiles across 33 cancer types. For each cancer type, we obtained transcriptomic, proteomic and phosphoproteomic measurements under both tumour and matched normal conditions. Expression values were standardized within each study and averaged across samples under the same condition. Let x i ∈ ℝ d denote the feature vector of gene i , where each dimension corresponds to the averaged expression value under a specific cancer type and condition. Specifically, for gene i , its expression in one cancer type is represented as:", "section_heading": "Implementation of XunZi-M", "supporting_figure_or_table": null}, {"route_id": "route_964334ebad2f", "route_label": "Neurodegenerative disease multi-omics feature learning", "lifecycle_phase": "pretraining", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "neurodegenerative disease", "source_object_verbatim": "AMP-AD, MSBB, ROSMAP, GEO and literature-derived multi-omics datasets for neurodegenerative diseases", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["derived the feature vector from its expression across distinct brain regions under different disease states", "embedded each gene node in the graph", "trained a GCN to propagate features across the heterogeneous graph structure"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "gene-level expression vectors", "carrier_family": "dense_continuous_carrier", "carrier_subtype": "direct_projected_embedding", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "embedded with an expression vector derived from the corresponding transcriptomic, proteomic or phosphoproteomic measurements under different biological conditions", "evidence_quote": "Building on the curated multi-omics datasets and the constructed knowledge graph, we developed disease-specific molecular pretrain models for pan-cancer and neurodegenerative diseases through graph-based representation learning. In both contexts, each node in the graph, representing a gene, was embedded with an expression vector derived from the corresponding transcriptomic, proteomic or phosphoproteomic measurements under different biological conditions. For each molecule identified in omics analyses, we calculated the following features: normalized transcriptomic expression denoted by fragments per kilobase of transcript per million mapped reads (FPKM) or transcripts per million (TPM) values for RNA sequencing (RNA-seq), normalized proteomic intensity (mass spectrometry-based protein quantification), mean phosphosite intensities (in cases where multiple p-sites were mapped to the same protein).", "section_heading": "Implementation of XunZi-M", "supporting_figure_or_table": null}, {"route_id": "route_639b574a31ba", "route_label": "Knowledge-graph node embedding", "lifecycle_phase": "pretraining", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "graph-based representation learning", "source_object_verbatim": "the complete knowledge graph comprising 621,152 nodes and 6,094,282 edges", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["collected protein entries from UniProt", "compiled PPI edges from public databases", "retrieved GO terms from the GO database", "constructed the complete knowledge graph", "embedded each node in a 4,096-dimensional vector"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "graph nodes and edges", "carrier_family": "dense_continuous_carrier", "carrier_subtype": "direct_projected_embedding", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "GCN over the heterogeneous graph structure", "evidence_quote": "To capture the molecular connectivity and functional landscape of the biological system, we constructed a large-scale protein-function knowledge graph by integrating protein identity with molecular interactions and functional annotations. We first collected 573,230 protein entries from UniProt 69 , which were mapped to standardized gene identifiers to establish the corresponding gene nodes. To capture physical and functional relationships between proteins, we compiled 2,813,799 PPI edges from seven public databases, including BioGRID 70 , iRefIndex 71 , PINA 72 , HINT 73 , mentha 74 , IID 75 and BioMap 76 . Meanwhile, we retrieved 47,922 GO terms from the GO database. On the basis of biological process annotations from UniProt, each protein was connected to one or more relevant GO terms, forming protein-GO associations. Using NetworkX v.2.5 (https://networkx.org/documentation/networkx-2.5/news.html), we constructed the complete knowledge graph comprising 621,152 nodes and 6,094,282 edges. In the entire graph, each node was embedded in a 4,096-dimensional vector to represent biological entities, including proteins and GO-defined functional terms, and edges capture either physical protein-protein interactions or functional annotations connecting proteins to their GO terms. This graph serves as the foundation for downstream knowledge representation and graph-based model training, enabling multimodal data fusion and integrated learning over both molecular structure and biological semantics.", "section_heading": "Construction of a knowledge graph for protein interactions and their involved biological processes", "supporting_figure_or_table": null}, {"route_id": "route_294185c05b89", "route_label": "PD-specific multi-omics refinement", "lifecycle_phase": "fine_tuning", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "predict PD-associated PKs", "source_object_verbatim": "PD-specific omics features", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["combined the pretrained node embeddings with PD-specific omics features", "trained a two-layer GCN to classify PD-relevant genes and kinases"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "pretrained node embeddings with PD-specific omics features", "carrier_family": "dense_continuous_carrier", "carrier_subtype": "pooled_or_aggregated_embedding", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "constructed a PD-specific subgraph by combining the pretrained node embeddings with PD-specific omics features", "evidence_quote": "Building on the curated multi-omics datasets and the constructed knowledge graph, we developed disease-specific molecular pretrain models for pan-cancer and neurodegenerative diseases through graph-based representation learning. In both contexts, each node in the graph, representing a gene, was embedded with an expression vector derived from the corresponding transcriptomic, proteomic or phosphoproteomic measurements under different biological conditions. For each molecule identified in omics analyses, we calculated the following features: normalized transcriptomic expression denoted by fragments per kilobase of transcript per million mapped reads (FPKM) or transcripts per million (TPM) values for RNA sequencing (RNA-seq), normalized proteomic intensity (mass spectrometry-based protein quantification), mean phosphosite intensities (in cases where multiple p-sites were mapped to the same protein).", "section_heading": "XunZi outperforms existing methods in predicting regulators underlying NSCLC and PD", "supporting_figure_or_table":